WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.895 --> 00:00:06.020
SEAN LEACH: Hey, everybody.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:06.590
Welcome.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.215
I'm going to be
talking today about how

00:00:08.215 --> 00:00:11.690
to achieve Zero Downtime
Migrations using Google Cloud

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.803
or GCP along with Fastly.

00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:14.720
My name is Sean Leach.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.090
I'm the chief product
officer here at Fastly,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.859
and I think it'll be a pretty
interesting conversation.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.120
So quick background.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.250
Fastly is an Edge Cloud.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.500
One of the well-known
products that

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.300
has been built on top of
this Edge Cloud is CDN.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.090
So we have quite a
few customers that

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.820
serve their content
and their application

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.140
globally around the world
using our very massive network.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.390
Not only can they serve
content, but they can actually

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.640
build pretty complex
applications on top of this

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.120
as well and take advantage
of that scale and reach.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.610
Our customers are kind of the
who's who out there, folks

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:59.600
like the New York Times, Airbnb,
Spotify, as well as jetBlue.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.330
So I want to talk today
about three strategies

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.069
around availability
and performance.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:09.140
The first one will be around
migrating from your data center

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.470
or other cloud
provider to GCP and how

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.520
you can use Fastly to do that.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.720
I'm also going to talk
about a newer topic

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.730
but a pretty fun topic
around microservice

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:20.980
routing on load balancing.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.480
You've probably heard things
like service meshes, et cetera.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.170
We'll talk a bit about that.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.710
Then we'll talk about
multi-cloud load balancing

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.830
strategies like how do you
use the Fastly global server

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.290
load-balancer between
cloud providers

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.260
to give you some advantages
on the performance side,

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.490
on the availability side, and
even on the cost side as well.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.270
So load balancing is
an interesting term.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.230
The name itself is
quite descriptive,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.850
but I think sometimes,
folks mistake load balancing

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.880
with failover, like, oh,
if this server fails,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.290
go to another server.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.940
That's a component
of load balancing,

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:59.260
but the main focus and
use of load balancing

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.620
is around distributing
your inbound traffic

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.470
across your computing resources.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.320
So if you have three
servers, and you

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.300
have all these customers
trying to get access

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.370
to your application,
how do you distribute

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.520
load across those servers
to best take advantage

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.850
of your infrastructure?

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.280
And then, if one of
those servers fails,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.340
then all the traffic
will be distributed

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.335
across the two servers.

00:02:22.335 --> 00:02:24.500
And then when that
server comes back up,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.360
it'll redistribute that traffic
across that third server.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.210
Traditional GSLB, and I've
been in this kind of space

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.290
a really long time.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.790
I always used DNS previously.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.360
So if you had
multiple data centers,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:42.860
multiple clouds, your DNS server
on every single DNS request

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.080
would make a decision
whether to route the end

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.730
user to one of your other
back end infrastructure.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.810
Now, the problem is is that
DNS queries only happen

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.040
once every so often, right?

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.070
You have this concept of
a TTL or a time-to-live

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:01.700
where a DNS request
will go from the client,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.110
an answer will be
given, and then

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.990
that answer will be cached
for anywhere from one minute

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.850
to a day.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:12.590
And so if you have failures
or your back ends become

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.330
overloaded, it's
very difficult to use

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.120
DNS as that routing
layer because it

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.790
doesn't respond quick enough.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.520
Another problem with
DNS is the DNS request

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.300
comes from a recursive server.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.140
It doesn't actually come
from the end user themselves.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.260
And a lot of times, if
you're using Google's DNS

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:36.620
or Cloudflare DNS or one of
these big anycast recursive DNS

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.160
farms, your queries are
lumped in with a whole bunch

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.430
of other people's queries.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:45.500
And so not only are you not
able to do load balancing

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.290
on a specific end
user level, you

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.830
aren't able to use
properties of the end user

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.980
as opposed to the DNS
server to make decisions.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.665
And I'll talk about that second.

00:03:57.665 --> 00:03:59.920
So Fastly GSLB is
quite a bit different.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.740
We're what's called a
layer 7 load balancer.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:06.970
And what we do is
when you make an HTTP

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.260
request to some resource that's
behind Fastly's load balancer,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.260
we can make a decision both
on every single request

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.035
because every HTTP request
goes through us as opposed

00:04:17.035 --> 00:04:19.089
to DNS, which gets cached.

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:24.760
But we also see the request come
in from the end client itself.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.090
So we allow our customers
to make really complex load

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:33.200
balancing decisions based on the
properties of the end client.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.697
So that could be a cookie value.

00:04:35.697 --> 00:04:37.030
It could be a particular header.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.630
It can be at the geo where
the end user is coming from,

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:45.430
what ISP they're using, as
well as you can take advantage

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.610
of if the back end, for example,
sets a particular cookie.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.760
Let's say you're a
bank, and you log in.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.830
And the bank sets
a cookie that says,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.390
this is one of our
high profile customers.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:59.800
A Layer 7 load balancer is
able to use that information

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.180
to make load
balancing decisions.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.660
Maybe they'll send it
to the bigger compute

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.745
farm, the faster
compute farm as opposed

00:05:06.745 --> 00:05:07.870
to the slower compute farm.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.703
Again, I have no idea
if they would do that,

00:05:09.703 --> 00:05:12.370
but that is a possibility.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.260
So let's talk a bit about cloud
microservice load balancing

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:17.430
strategies.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.892
You really want it to be
infrastructure agnostic,

00:05:19.892 --> 00:05:22.100
and then, you want to have
a unified approach, right?

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.930
If you're using multiple
clouds, and they're usually

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.200
different providers,
it's nice to have

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.900
a single source of all
of your load balancing

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.820
decisions and infrastructure.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.622
So I'm going to cover some
of that in our demos today.

00:05:35.622 --> 00:05:39.000
But one of the beauties-- and
I'll talk a bit about this

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.490
as well-- around
multi-cloud is the ability

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.390
to take advantage
of the strengths

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.675
and avoid the weaknesses of
different cloud providers.

00:05:46.675 --> 00:05:48.300
And so I'll talk a
little bit about how

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.716
you can use our load balancer
to take advantage of that.

00:05:52.716 --> 00:05:56.750
So again, I talked a bit
about the functionality

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:57.830
that we provide.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.020
The key points
are content-aware.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.910
We can look at the URL
the end user's going to,

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.340
the properties of the end
user, and we can make decisions

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.180
based on that, whether
it's a microservice or just

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:12.710
a normal monolithic application.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.750
Infrastructure agnostic design.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.280
We allow you to have
a single place where

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.650
you can configure your routing
and your load balancing rules.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.780
You don't have to have different
rules for different cloud

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:24.320
providers.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:25.070
It doesn't matter.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.520
As long as the back end
speaks HTTP, you can use it.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.960
Immediate control and insights.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.490
Like a big part
of load balancing

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:35.140
is understanding right away
the health of your back ends,

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.250
how the load
balancing is working

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.420
in terms of what
level of load it's

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.750
sending to this server
versus that server.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.480
With our system, we give you
full and real-time access

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.970
to all of the logs
on every single hit

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.460
to the load balancer, which
allows you to build some really

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.610
cool analytics and
observability back end using

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.940
your favorite infrastructure
analytics provider.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:57.410
And then, Scalability.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.990
We're a massive-- many, many,
many tens of terabits a second,

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:04.860
tons of locations
around the world.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.540
We instantly scale up.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.710
So you don't have to worry about
some of the cold start problems

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.230
that you've gotten
with other load

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.850
balancing solutions or having
to buy a bunch of hardware

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:18.050
and support it like you've
done for the past many years.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.690
So let's look into
Use Case Number 1.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:23.420
You're in a data
center, as a lot of you

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:26.900
have been in the past,
and now, as a lot of folks

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.750
want to do in the future,
you want to move to GCP.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.340
We're huge fans of GCP.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.640
We use GCP for a lot of our
control plane technology.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.070
And so we can definitely see
why you would want to do this.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.440
So let's talk a bit about this.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.670
Why would you do it?

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.000
I think it's pretty
straightforward.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.500
You want to move
to Google Cloud,

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.920
take advantage of all
the benefits of cloud--

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:53.530
scalability, analytics, control.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.460
But what do you
want to do as you

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:58.700
move to the cloud is you want
to make it seamless to your end

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:00.010
users, right?

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:04.190
You don't want to redirect
them to a different server

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:05.990
so that they see the
browser redirect.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.840
That can have a lot of problems
when it comes to performance.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.760
You want to make it as
seamless as possible

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.570
to move all of your traffic
from a data center provider

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:15.690
to a cloud provider.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.170
And that's where a Layer
7 load balancer really

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:23.000
comes into its own in that
because you have a single DNS

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.470
name that everybody sees--

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.463
let's just use www.example.com--

00:08:27.463 --> 00:08:29.130
in the layers that
have a load balancer,

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.310
you can be having
all your traffic

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.159
to go to your
primary data center.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:38.780
And then, over time,
you could do it via geo.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:41.809
You can do it based on
customers that have opted in.

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:43.730
You can send a percentage
of your traffic

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:46.100
to your new cloud
infrastructure.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.410
And that way, your
end users actually

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.500
never see whether they're going
to a data center or a cloud.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.890
All they see is www.example.com,
and behind the scenes,

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.570
you're doing all
the work to make

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.730
it really easy and
straightforward to move them

00:08:58.730 --> 00:08:59.295
over.

00:08:59.295 --> 00:09:00.920
And then, if there's
a problem, and you

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.090
want to move back to the
data center, it's instant.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.450
You don't have to wait for
DNS to propagate, which again

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.460
can take up to a day sometimes.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:11.160
So it's a really
powerful way of doing it.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.790
And as a provider that's for
developers, by developers,

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:18.450
of course, we let everything
that you do be programmable.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.490
Our entire UI, it
only uses the API.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.250
We're an API-first company.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:26.430
We allow you use Terraform as
well if that's the solution

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.690
that you like to use.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.120
But what you can
see here is this

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.400
is just a simple
API call that allows

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.883
you to create a pool
that is one for GCP

00:09:37.883 --> 00:09:39.300
and then one for
your data center.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.360
And then once it's activated,
within a second or two

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:44.370
globally, that change happens.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.187
So you don't have
to make a change

00:09:46.187 --> 00:09:48.270
and then wait for it to
propagate for half an hour

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.890
for that traffic
to start moving.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.140
It's an instant change
because of how quickly you

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:53.580
can push config changes out.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.800
That was one of our earliest
competitive differentiators was

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.720
not only could you invalidate
content instantly--

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:05.070
which allowed people to put
real-time inventory behind us

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.080
or rapidly changing home pages--

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.530
we also allowed you to push
your config changes out

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.060
within a second globally.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:13.890
That changed the game
on what you could

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:17.710
do with a CDN or an Edge Cloud.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.550
So this is where you can create
multiple load balancers based

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.290
on different IP addresses.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:26.250
So you can do local load
balancing as well or things

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.990
like service mesh, which
I'll talk about in a bit.

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:30.510
But it gives you
that full power.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.600
You can have
different data centers

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.150
that you send your
traffic between,

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:38.640
but then you can actually
have different load balancing

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.350
algorithms between servers
within those data centers,

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.600
which is really powerful.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.340
And then because
Fastly was originally

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.490
based on a highly modified
version of Varnish,

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:55.950
which is one of the most popular
caching pieces of open source

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.220
software out there.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.180
We use a language called VCL
to do your configuration.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.330
And so you can see here that you
can do a random load balancer

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:04.650
just by setting the back end.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:05.970
And you can fully program this.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.780
It's not like you only can use
a UI to make your decisions.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:12.360
You have full
programmatic access

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.180
to configuring
the load balancer.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.520
The classic example of some
folks that have used this--

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.810
there is probably the biggest
radio provider out there.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:24.420
They had to quickly move
out of their data center

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:26.150
and into the cloud.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.270
Their data center provider
had basically said,

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:29.410
you've got a week.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:31.330
So using our load
balancer, they didn't

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:33.460
have to buy any new
gear or anything.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.360
They just use our SAS
code base load balancer.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.150
They slowly were able to
migrate that radio traffic

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.170
from the data center
into the cloud,

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.665
and there was no
visible artifact

00:11:43.665 --> 00:11:44.790
of that to their end users.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.550
Their end users had no
idea that they had moved.

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.830
They just got a lot
faster all of a sudden.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.930
And Vimeo is another
big customer of ours.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.100
They used this to
move from NetStorage,

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:58.710
which was a storage provider
of their previous CDN to using

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.450
GCS as their storage.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.870
So they got to take advantage
of all the amazingness of GCS--

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.480
which, again, we're
huge fans of GCS.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.530
And then, they were able to
then move that traffic off

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.480
of their old legacy
system into GCS.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.300
So they used a really
cool feature of ours

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.270
as well where we would
actually check GCS first

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:19.620
as part of the move.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.120
And if it wasn't there, if
it hadn't been migrated yet,

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:23.820
we'd go to the previous
storage system.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:28.132
Again, all behind the scenes
unknown to the end user.

00:12:28.132 --> 00:12:30.340
So let's talk about Use Case
2, microservices, right?

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:33.480
It's the new hotness, service
mesh and microservices.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:36.280
And now, I'm even hearing this
thing called macroservices.

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.400
So the IT world was
always changing,

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:40.660
but it's always a
lot of fun as well.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.470
Let's talk about
service-oriented architecture

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:44.810
or microservices routing.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:48.810
So if you think about it
from a conceptual level,

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:53.220
it's very similar to multi-cloud
or multi-data center,

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.990
but instead, you
have multi-services.

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:58.600
And again, HTTP is the
language of the web.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:01.170
And so as long as
everything speaks HTTP,

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.640
it's really simple.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.650
So what you can do is you can
break that big monolithic up

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:10.680
into smaller sub-services
or microservices,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.690
and then you can
use Fastly to route

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.710
based on the proper service
based on the URL path, right?

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:21.210
So we have a lot of customers
who have /images will go

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:24.990
to the image service, which
may be hosted in the same data

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.120
center or different cloud
provider, et cetera.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.670
Then you may have a
Kubernetes service

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.220
that's running like your
shopping cart application.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:37.980
So we can route anything that
goes to /cart or /product

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.930
to that particular app.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:42.600
And then you might
have a third party

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.520
integration with some GraphQL
API or something like that.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.830
So /api can go to a
different service.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:52.650
And again, it's all done
on every single request

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.390
with the Layer 7 level.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.490
And if any of those
fails, we could instantly

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.560
failover to a backup provider.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:02.880
Gannett who's one of the
biggest publishers out there--

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.460
they own USA Today and
a ton of newspapers--

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:09.000
they use Fastly to route to all
their back end microservices.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.420
And the other beauty
of Fastly because we're

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:14.370
100% both real-time
and API control,

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:17.160
they can integrate all this
into their CICD workflow.

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:20.310
So they don't have to have all
their testing and deployment

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.520
pipeline using the CICD
system, and then they're

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.280
also their load balancer,
their CDN service.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.230
Everything is
fully programmable.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.600
So every change that
they make, the test

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.790
is done based on their
cloud infrastructure

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:35.950
and the configuration
on Fastly in real time.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.270
So they know right away
if something's going on.

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:44.330
You look here, very similar
API for the microservices side

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:47.550
where you have an images service
and you have a CMS service.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.190
And then you might have--

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:54.940
here's some example VCL as well,
where, again, based on the URL,

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.300
you can see /images right there.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.620
That would send to a
different back end,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.170
which is the images server.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:04.680
And certain things, we may
have to modify the request URL

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.810
or drain the post body.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:09.120
There's all sorts of
cool stuff you can do

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:11.640
using the power of this system.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.670
And then, the other really
cool thing about this

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:17.050
is these endpoints can be
scaled independently as well.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:21.080
So the images-- if you have
multiple image providers

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.270
you're looking at using like
image optimization providers,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.710
you can test all of
those completely,

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:29.260
transparently to your end users.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:32.090
You can send a third of
your traffic to each one

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:35.330
and see which one's
faster using our logging.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:39.470
You can see which one provides
a better conversion percentage

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:41.870
because you could set a
cookie or a header that says

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:43.820
which image service you chose.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:46.190
And then your analytic
system could show that to you

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:50.240
and say, oh, provider A, I
got way better conversion

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:50.900
using that.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:55.040
So a lot of our customers use us
for some really cool AB testing

00:15:55.040 --> 00:16:00.410
solutions as well or
green-blue deployments.

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.470
Last Use Case I want
to talk about a bit

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:03.860
is just multi-cloud.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:05.850
So you've moved out
of the data center.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:07.920
You're using microservices.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:11.250
You're pretty proud of yourself,
of the work that you've done.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:15.200
Now, you're finding that you're
using just one cloud provider

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:17.330
out there.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:18.830
There's a few
challenges with that.

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:21.050
I know a ton of
customers that are

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:22.890
cloud native with
a single provider,

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:24.110
and they're very happy.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:26.090
But then, I hear
quite a bit from CFOs

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:31.340
that they don't like being so
dependent on a single provider,

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:33.500
both from a
availability perspective

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.380
or a monetary perspective.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:40.490
One of the beauties of
the cloud is cost savings

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:42.900
and cost optimization.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.040
So with multi-cloud,
you may be sitting on--

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.890
here's a graph or
a good diagram.

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:51.590
It talks about you may be a big
Amazon Web Services customer

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:52.880
and you say, you know what?

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.830
I've heard some amazing
things about Google.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.540
I've heard they've got
amazing analytics tools.

00:16:57.540 --> 00:16:59.240
They've got really
great technology.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:01.160
I mean, that's why we're
huge Google fans is

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:04.369
both on the data side and
just the technology side.

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:09.210
You want to start moving and
taking advantage of Google,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:09.710
right?

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.170
Maybe, it's you
want to do failover

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:13.540
between Amazon and Google.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.290
It may be that you're
like, you know what?

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.920
I want to start moving to
Google fully as my primary,

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.750
but I want to do
it in slow order

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:22.970
and maybe I keep
AWS as a backup.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:25.349
We have quite a few customers
like Shazam and Taboola

00:17:25.349 --> 00:17:28.730
who have done very
similar things where they

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:30.830
want flexibility and options.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:34.220
And so with us because of
our load balancer-- again,

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:37.080
completely transparent
to the user--

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.360
you can use multiple
cloud providers

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:42.230
and even take advantage of
the strengths of each one.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.870
So data analytics Google
is so good at that.

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:46.940
You might send some of
your analytics components

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:48.320
of your app through Google.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:50.050
But maybe you've
got this legacy app

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.050
that you've built on
Amazon that takes advantage

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:54.020
of a lot of their tech.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:57.070
You send the traffic for
some of that to the Amazon

00:17:57.070 --> 00:18:02.120
back ends, completely
behind www.example.com.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:05.390
A lot of our customers,
they use their www record

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:09.050
as this single ingress
point for all traffic

00:18:09.050 --> 00:18:12.488
so that they can not just
serve traffic and load balance

00:18:12.488 --> 00:18:14.530
traffic to different back
ends, but maybe they've

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:17.930
acquired technology over time
that they need to integrate.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:21.410
Again, that www record
becomes that universal source,

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.720
and it also becomes
a great audit point.

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:27.440
So they can tell what all of
their applications and APIs

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.930
are by logging every hit to www.

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:33.200
And not allowing any
traffic to anything but www,

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:36.635
it becomes a pretty
amazing audit system.

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:43.440
And then like multi-cloud
geographic load balance,

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.620
it's really cool to be
able to take advantage

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:51.870
of sending customers to the
locations closest to them,

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:52.370
right?

00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:55.340
You may have many
Google locations where

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.380
you're running applications.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.660
And instead of just
randomly sending traffic

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:03.520
to different
locations, if an end

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.570
user's coming from
New York, you want

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.580
to send to the New
York Google location.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.080
If they're coming
from Virginia, you

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:10.860
want to send them to the
Virginia Google location.

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:12.050
If they're coming
from California,

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:14.190
you want to send them to
the California location.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:20.030
So we geo-stamp as well as have
IP and ASN or autonomous system

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:21.740
information about
every single request

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.900
that we expose to the
user, so that they

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:28.920
can send the traffic to the
closest end user location.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.430
And then, we health-check
all the different locations.

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:34.063
So in case one data
center is taken out

00:19:34.063 --> 00:19:35.480
for maintenance
or whatever it is,

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.520
we can reroute all
that traffic instantly

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.680
to a different location.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.450
So here's an example here.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:44.390
Based on a certain path, we
create these health checks

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:48.080
that are constantly checking
a particular location

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:49.320
or a particular IP.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:51.250
And it's all done
again by our API.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:52.310
et cetera.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:56.630
And then here's some logic
on the load balancer side

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:57.600
that talks about--

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.380
it looks at the server region
and it looks at the back end

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:03.020
and it makes different decisions
based on those properties

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:06.110
to send you to the
proper location.

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:06.710
So that's it.

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:09.710
Hopefully, it was helpful.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:11.570
We expose all of
our documentation

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:13.430
online, if you go
to docs.fastly.com.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:16.760
We've got an amazing developer
portal at developer.fastly.com

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:18.120
you can take a look at.

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:20.300
I'm happy to talk as well.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.040
If you ever have
any questions of me,

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:24.110
it's just sean@fastly.com,
but I really

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:26.770
appreciate you taking
the time today.

